<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    Repo-scoped feed list. <clear /> is deliberate: without it NuGet inherits
    parent-directory configs, and a Windows-only local source (e.g. D:\...\Packages)
    breaks restore when the same tree is built from WSL/Linux.
    dotnet-eng is required by the upstream build, see README.md "Getting Started".
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    <add key="dotnet-eng" value="https://pkgs.dev.azure.com/dnceng/public/_packaging/dotnet-eng/nuget/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    <!--
      The Net4x.* Gtk 4 bindings this backend is built on. They are produced by the GtkSharp
      repository (github.com/pieroviano/GtkSharp, branch net4x.gtk4) and land in Packages/, which
      on a development machine is a junction onto that repository's BuildOutput/NugetPackages drop
      folder - so `dotnet cake build.cake` there publishes straight into this feed, with no copy
      step and no intermediate publish.

      The path is RELATIVE, and that is the difference from the case the note above rules out:
      NuGet resolves it against this file's own directory, so it is correct from Windows and from
      a /mnt/d working copy under WSL alike. (A WSL clone living on the Linux filesystem cannot
      see the junction at all - build from /mnt/d there.)

      Packages/ is gitignored (.gitignore:32) and cannot be committed: it is a reparse point into
      a shared drop folder. CI materialises it by building the GtkSharp repository - see
      docs/plans/gtk4-migration.md V1. A missing folder is only NU1801 (a warning) as far as NuGet
      is concerned, which would silently fall through to nuget.org; Xamarin.Forms.Platform.GTK's
      _XFAssertLocalFeedExists turns that into a build error.
    -->
    <add key="local-net4x" value="Packages" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
